  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Type 2 diabetes is diagnosed using specific blood tests that measure the amount of glucose (sugar) in your blood.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "using specific blood tests that measure the amount of glucose (sugar) in your blood"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "According to guidelines from organizations like the American Diabetes Association , doctors use four main diagnostic blood tests.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American Diabetes Association",
            "link": "https://diabetes.org/about-diabetes/diagnosis"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Diagnostic Blood Tests"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "A1C Test (Glycated Hemoglobin): This test shows your average blood sugar level over the past two to three months. You do not need to fast.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diagnosis: A result of 6.5% or higher means you have diabetes."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Fasting Blood Sugar Test: This test measures your blood sugar after you do not eat or drink anything except water for at least eight hours, usually overnight.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diagnosis: A result of 126 mg/dL (milligrams per deciliter) or higher indicates diabetes."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: This test measures your blood sugar before and two hours after you drink a special sweet liquid containing 75 grams of glucose.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diagnosis: A level of 200 mg/dL or higher after two hours confirms diabetes."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Random Blood Sugar Test: This test checks your blood sugar at any time of day, regardless of when you last ate. It is typically used if you have active symptoms.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diagnosis: A level of 200 mg/dL or higher paired with common symptoms (such as extreme thirst or frequent urination) suggests diabetes."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Confirming the Diagnosis"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Except for a random test with clear symptoms, doctors usually repeat the same test or a different test on a separate day to confirm a diabetes diagnosis before starting treatment. You can learn more about testing procedures from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention",
            "link": "https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/diabetes-testing/index.html"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Would you like to share what symptoms or risk factors you are experiencing so I can help you prepare questions for your doctor?"
      }
    ],
